WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:17.160
Jakob Fugger: The Richest Man Who Ever Lived

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.580
For as long as mankind has had currency, a
system of trading, and a market economy, the

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:27.410
world has been split into the "haves" and
the "have-nots," … and for much of that

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.550
time, there's been a huge gap between the
two.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:35.610
The idea of the 1 percent isn't a new concept
by any means, and in order to talk about one

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.860
of the richest men in history, we'll have
to go all the way back to the 15th century.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:46.710
The man who would become known as Jakob Fugger
the Rich was born in 1459, and he had an advantage

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.520
that few people get, then or now -- he was
born into a well-to-do family.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.250
But through a series of investments made with
incredible foresight and a willingness to

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:00.870
risk it all, Fugger guided his family to become
one of the richest and most influential in

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:01.870
history.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:02.880
Here's how he did it.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:10.240
Just how Rich is Rich?

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.171
Since we're going to be talking about Jakob
Fugger in the context of his wealth, it's

00:01:14.171 --> 00:01:17.110
worth investigating just how rich he really
was.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:22.420
Perspective is key, after all, so let's compare
his fortune to that of people who might be

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.040
a little more familiar.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.040
Take Bill Gates.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.450
His highest net worth hit a seriously impressive
$144 billion, although he gives so much money

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.869
away through his foundation, that's not really
an accurate picture.

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:37.220
Go back in time about 200 years, and we have
John Jacob Astor.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:42.050
He has the honor of being America's first
multi-millionaire; he controlled much of the

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:46.390
fur trade in the early 19th century, and when
he died, he was worth what would be around

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.280
$168 billion in today's money.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.189
Henry Ford tips the scales at around $200
billion in today's money, which puts him right

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:58.369
alongside names like Cornelius Vanderbilt
and Muammar Gaddafi.

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:03.620
Rockfeller and Carnegie come in somewhere
in the middle of the $300-billion range.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.759
Fugger was richer than all of them.

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:09.660
There's a few different ways you can look
at wealth, and given the fast-changing modern

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.270
economy, plus factoring in inflation, a simple
numerical comparison doesn't really carry

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.270
much weight.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:20.500
It's estimated that in today's US currency,
Fugger would have a net worth of somewhere

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.660
around $400 billion, but there's so much more
to it than that.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.030
Consider Fugger in a grander context.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:33.790
If you took Europe's entire economic output
at the time, he owned 2.2 percent of that.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:38.090
If that doesn’t sound like much, remember:
we’re talking about representing more than

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.860
two percent of an entire continent.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:47.830
For reference, examine John D. Rockefeller’s
value -- when he died in 1937, he owned only

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:51.040
1.5 percent of America's gross domestic product.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.010
And that's just one country.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.570
But before you get bogged down by dollar signs,
don’t forget -- we aren’t just talking

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.180
about the value of money.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:04.510
Fugger wielded a great deal of power and influence,
and that can be the most valuable currency

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.940
of all.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.633
The benefits of a privileged upbringing

00:03:11.633 --> 00:03:15.830
Jakob the Rich may have been the economic
savant who catapulted the family fortune to

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:20.380
newfound heights, but the Fugger fortune wasn't
built on the shoulders of one man.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.240
There are a few others in the family that
deserve an honorable mention.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.500
Jakob's grandfather, Hans, was born into a
peasant family in southern Germany.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.670
By 1367, he had moved to Augsburg and made
a few wise choices in the game of love.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:38.390
He married twice, and both of his wives were
the daughters of masters in the weaver's guild.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:42.330
That's not to say he was a master weaver himself,
though; Hans didn't weave at all.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:47.100
What he did do was act as the middleman, providing
raw materials for those who performed the

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.100
manual labor.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:51.850
Later, he would buy the finished textiles
and sell them at a massive profit.

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:55.900
From there, he moved up in the guild himself,
earning a spot on the committee, and eventually

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.030
serving on the Great Council of Augsburg.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:02.710
Hans died in 1408, but he didn't pass the
business on to his sons; instead, his wife

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:08.480
Elizabeth took the reins, and spent the next
28 years using her own business savvy to continue

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.200
growing the family’s wealth.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.910
Hans had two sons of note, Andreas and Jakob
I, and eventually, they made their own fortunes

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.720
and contributed to the family's.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:23.790
Both were goldsmiths, and they worked together
until disagreements led to a split in 1454.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:28.630
Even though Andreas had more immediate success,
that branch of the family was bankrupt by

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:29.630
1499.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:34.300
Jakob, on the other hand, increased his wealth
and standing slowly but surely.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.470
He married the daughter of a mint master,
and they raised seven sons — all while he

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.880
grew his status as a member of the city's
merchants' guild.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.690
Two of his sons were groomed to take over
the financial side of the business.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:51.270
The youngest — Jakob II, our future Jakob
the Rich — was, like many youngest sons

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.600
were, on his way to being educated for a place
in the clergy.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.980
Jakob — also known as Jakob the Elder — died
in 1469.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:04.090
For the next 28 years, once again, the business
affairs weren't in the hands of any of those

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:07.850
sons, but were managed by the deceased’s
wife, Barbara Basinger.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:12.380
She spent nearly three decades buying and
selling, and when it came time to hand control

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.690
over to the next generation, the family was
already quite wealthy.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:21.570
It was Barbara who sent her youngest son off
not just to ecclesiastical studies, but to

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:23.570
learn banking and bookkeeping as well.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:29.000
Jakob II was just a teenager when he was sent
to Venice to learn the ins and outs of commerce

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.480
in a major European city; by the time he came
back, he had learned skills that would serve

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.480
him well.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.620
He studied not just the principles of trading
and commerce, but he also learned how to read,

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.430
write, and speak multiple languages.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:44.690
He also mastered more obscure things, like
the formulas for making conversions between

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.211
various weights, measures, and currencies.

00:05:47.211 --> 00:05:48.540
And then, there was accounting.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.500
The idea is common enough today, but at the
time, German merchants still relied heavily

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.560
on a haphazard system of receipts.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.540
Organization was rarely included as a part
of a German merchant's job description, but

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.460
things were quite different in Italy.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:05.690
There, they had developed extensive systems
of bookkeeping, an accounting procedure that

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:10.780
made it possible to stay on top of multinational
companies and trade that spanned the length

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.020
of the Silk Road.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:41.350
Jakob was going to need to know how to do
all of it.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:07:14.310
A First Taste of Wealth and Power

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:44.770
It

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:48.710
was several years before Jakob returned to
Germany, and by that time, his older brother

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:50.790
had already set up a firm of their own.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:56.710
It was called Ulrich Fugger &amp; Bros., and Jakob
started out with a 29 percent ownership stake.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.340
He wasn't working with them for long when
he witnessed a deal that made a lasting impact

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:04.960
on him — and taught him a very important
lesson about the makings of a king.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.680
Ulrich Fugger was approached by the Habsburg
Emperor Frederick III.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:15.520
He was installed as Holy Roman emperor in
1452, and his reign was both turbulent and

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.000
historically significant.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:21.960
He dealt with a series of revolts and revolutions,
as well as continued unrest on the eastern

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:23.290
border of his kingdom.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:27.710
He was on the front lines of the long-running
battle between the Ottoman Empire and Christian

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:31.770
Europe, maintaining a firm relationship with
the church, but struggling to consolidate

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.169
his territory into a unified power.

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:38.770
One of the most important things he's still
known for isn't necessarily what he did, but

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:40.680
what he laid the groundwork for.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.820
In 1477, he made a deal to join his family
with the Duke of Burgundy's via the marriage

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.180
of his son to the daughter of Charles the
Bold.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:53.070
Before the marriage could be agreed upon,
though, Frederick needed to impress the Burgundians

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:54.830
to prove his family’s worthiness.

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:57.940
With the coffers running dry, he turned to
the Fuggers.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:03.180
Frederick III found himself in dire need of
fine textiles, and that was precisely what

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.860
the Fugger family did best.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:09.470
Jakob watched as his older brother made the
deal: he would give the Holy Roman Emperor

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:14.630
the fine silks and wool he needed to impress
Charles, but he wanted something in return.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:18.890
That was a coat of arms, bestowed upon all
the Fugger brothers.

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:23.210
It doesn't sound like much, but for the family,
it was a huge deal that allowed them to rise

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:28.560
from just being well-to-do members of the
merchant class to being a bona fide family

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.040
of standing and clout.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.310
It was a win-win — the deal also helped
Frederick to close on the marriage proposal,

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:39.660
which, in turn, consolidated power and made
Austria a force to be reckoned with.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:44.740
From all of this, Jakob learned that even
kings would bend the knee before money.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:49.820
Every haggle might come with some natural
give and take, but a careful negotiation could

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.880
have a very far-reaching outcome, indeed.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:55.900
In 1485, Jakob made his first solo deal.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:10:00.710
The 26-year old was still working for his
brothers when he headed off to Schwaz, Austria,

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:03.160
with a desire to break into the mining industry.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:07.350
His brothers had given him full authority
to make any business decisions he deemed appropriate,

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:10.589
which turned out to be one of the better decisions
that the family made.

00:10:10.589 --> 00:10:15.150
Jakob met with Sigismund, the Archduke of
Austria and ruler of Tyrol.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:20.080
Prior to his elevation to these particular
titles, Sigismund was a ward of his cousin,

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.780
the aforementioned Emperor Frederick III.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:27.540
When he was given regency over Tyrol and several
other territories, he was handed incredibly

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.420
valuable lands, rich with silver mines.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:34.470
And while Sigismund may have been instrumental
in promoting and growing his country's mining

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:39.720
industry, he also liked to spend money as
fast as it was mined.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:44.460
His love of extravagance earned him the epithet
"Rich in Coin," but he wasn't always able

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.590
to keep up with his spending.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:49.280
Enter Jakob Fugger.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.440
He had the extra cash that Sigismund so desperately
needed, so they struck an intriguing deal

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:59.260
-- Jakob agreed to lend the Archduke a sizeable
sum, and in return, the Fuggers would gain

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.470
the rights to the area's silver mines.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:06.060
Money up front in return for the possibility
of money in the future seemed like a brilliant

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:10.840
trade, so Sigismund took it in exchange for
a thousand pounds of silver, delivered over

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.000
several installments.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.959
He did, in fact, deliver the mined silver
as promised, which wasn't always a guarantee

00:11:15.959 --> 00:11:20.710
— as an Archduke, could have just leveraged
his own power and standing to decide he wasn't

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:22.680
going to bother with repayment.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:26.770
Fortunately for the Fuggers, the deliveries
came in right on time, and Jakob was able

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:30.140
to sell that same silver in Venice at a 50
percent markup.

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.410
And that's what we call good business.

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:37.170
The Fugger fortune didn't come from that thousand
pounds of silver, but that first deal did

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:42.200
something else — it cemented a relationship
between the Habsburg dynasty and the Fugger

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:48.080
banking empire, because just four years later,
Sigismund found himself in dire need of more

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:49.080
help.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:52.730
This time, his troubles started with the outbreak
of a border dispute between Innsbruck and

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:53.730
Venice.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:57.040
Frankly, calling it a "border dispute" is
putting it rather mildly.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.610
After noticing that the Venician military
occupied in a fight with Turkish forces in

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:06.959
Greece, Sigismund launched a modest opportunistic
attack, claiming a small Alpine town and extending

00:12:06.959 --> 00:12:08.270
the Austrian borders.

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:11.680
It probably goes without saying that Venice
wasn't terribly happy about this.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.430
They issued their response in no uncertain
terms: Sigismund could either return the town

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:21.070
and pay 100,000 florins in restitution for
his brovado, or he could prepare for Venice

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:23.500
to take back their town by force.

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:27.170
Sigismund appealed to the family's regular
financiers, but no one was willing to lend

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:28.170
him the money.

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:31.830
They refused not because of his military action,
but because he had already managed to run

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:34.060
up massive debts that he hadn't yet repaid.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:36.370
Desperate, he returned to Jakob Fugger.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:39.360
Where other bankers saw a risk, Jakob saw
an opportunity.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.650
He agreed to lend Sigismund the money he needed,
but the interest on the deal was going to

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:44.650
be massive.

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:48.830
The contract stated that the loan was going
to be repaid with more silver from Sigismund's

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:53.660
mines — all output was to be sold to the
Fuggers at an almost unthinkable discount,

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:55.080
until the loan was repaid.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.930
The Fuggers could then turn around and sell
that silver, and they stood to make what could

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:01.950
safely be described as boatloads of cash.

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:03.980
The deal wasn't without risks.

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:07.520
Sigismund had already proven he couldn't repay
his regular bankers, and there was the very

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.970
real possibility he would simply stop sending
silver to the Fuggers.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:15.740
But Jakob had banked on the fact that this
was a bridge Sigismund didn't want to burn,

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:20.260
gambling that he would uphold his end of the
deal in order to preserve the possibility

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:21.710
of future partnerships.

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:26.279
It was a little bit about honor, and a lot
about knowing he would need to borrow money

00:13:26.279 --> 00:13:30.560
again in the future, and it all came up shiny
for Jakob.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:34.630
So shiny, in fact, that the Fuggers opened
their first branch office in Tyrol, and it's

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:39.990
important to stress that this was a gamble
on more than just Sigismund's ability or desire

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:41.550
to uphold his end of the bargain.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:45.890
Metallurgy had been a work in progress for
thousands of years; the evolution of the ages

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:50.540
of man are defined by advancements in the
use of materials like bronze and iron, after

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:51.540
all.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:55.980
At the time, Jakob was making deals for silver,
but the corresponding smelting technologies

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.030
had stalled.

00:13:57.030 --> 00:14:01.290
Starting around 1480, though, metallurgical
advancements began happening in leaps and

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:02.290
bounds.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:05.380
It was becoming much more economical to separate
silver from other metals.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:09.180
In turn, the demand for not silver, lead,
mercury, and copper exploded.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:13.190
Portugal, in particular, was buying huge amounts
of copper for their ships and their weapons,

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:16.310
and the Fugger were well-positioned to capitalize
on all of it.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:20.620
In 1494, Jakob formed the family’s first
public company, a mining consortium.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:25.130
His goal was a copper monopoly; to get there,
he started investing heavily in copper mining,

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:29.230
buying everything from mines to smelting plants
across present-day Slovakia.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:33.360
It's impossible to tell just how massive this
portion of their industry was, as they were

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:38.089
incredibly secretive when it came to the technology
they used and the numbers they saw floating

00:14:38.089 --> 00:14:39.089
across their accounts.

00:14:39.089 --> 00:14:42.880
We do know that it wasn't long before they
had built up a massive trading empire that

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:45.450
stretched from England to India and Africa.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:51.399
It was an industry that sustained the family
wealth well into the 16th century.

00:14:51.399 --> 00:14:57.330
Bankers to the rich, the noble, and the elite

00:14:57.330 --> 00:15:01.130
In 1491, Jakob had made another wise decision
— he organized a loan to Maxmilian I, the

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:02.610
son of Frederick III.

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:07.540
When Maximilian became a ruler in his own
right after his father's death in 1493, that

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:12.800
cemented Jakob's continued standing as a banker
to the powerful and not-so-rich men of the

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:14.890
15th and 16th centuries.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:18.800
That — alongside the aforementioned investments
into a burgeoning mining industry — meant

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.730
that the Fugger family coffers were filling
fast.

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:24.490
Still, Jakob was always on the lookout for
the next big deal.

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:26.529
The trades came in faster and faster.

00:15:26.529 --> 00:15:29.480
By 1503, they had gotten into the lucrative
spice trade.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:33.051
Only five years later, they were leasing mints
in Rome, and among the coins they were in

00:15:33.051 --> 00:15:35.160
charge of minting were those for the Vatican.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:40.370
They kept that particular little side business
until 1524, and here's where it's worth mentioning

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.730
one endeavor in particular.

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:46.990
In 1514, one of the most powerful positions
in the church and in the political world became

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:47.990
available.

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:52.830
It was for the archbishop of Mainz, a high-ranking
position in Germany second only to the emperor

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:53.830
himself.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:58.459
The pope at the time was Leo X, and while
that name might not sound particularly familiar,

00:15:58.459 --> 00:16:01.890
his birth name will: Giovanni de Medici.

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:05.740
His reign as pope is still known as one of
the most extravagant — he was known as a

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:08.470
patron of the arts, and that's putting it
politely.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:13.600
Elected to the papacy in 1513 at 37 years
of age, he made it his mission to elevate

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:15.769
the Vatican into something divine.

00:16:15.769 --> 00:16:20.010
In order to collect the enormous sum of money
he needed to renovate the holy city, he started

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:25.589
doing some shady things that most devout Catholics
absolutely didn't agree with, like selling

00:16:25.589 --> 00:16:27.680
holy positions within the church.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:32.020
The position of the archbishop of Mainz was
one that was up for sale, and anyone who wanted

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:35.610
to buy their way into the seat was going to
have to pay up.

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:36.610
How much?

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:40.990
Adjusted for inflation, it would cost the
equivalent of about $4.8 million in today's

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:41.990
money.

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:46.259
It was Albrecht of Hohenzollern who was determined
to have it, and he turned to Jakob Fugger

00:16:46.259 --> 00:16:47.259
for financing.

00:16:47.259 --> 00:16:51.160
Jakob agreed, and he deposited the money right
into the pope's personal bank account.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:54.480
Hey, you’ve at least got to admire the openness
of it all, right?

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:58.540
Albrecht got the position, but now he had
a hefty loan he needed to repay.

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:00.830
And how on earth was he going to do that?

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:06.230
Like most of Jakob's deals, it was complicated
— and there were a few players in place.

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:11.019
With the support of the pope, Albrecht started
selling indulgences, or get-out-of-jail free

00:17:11.019 --> 00:17:12.409
cards for Heaven.

00:17:12.409 --> 00:17:16.289
The idea was that anyone who was willing to
make a sizeable “donation" to the church

00:17:16.289 --> 00:17:21.579
would have their sins immediately erased,
and their way into Heaven paved… presumably,

00:17:21.579 --> 00:17:24.009
with the silver and copper from Jakob's mines.

00:17:24.009 --> 00:17:28.169
Perhaps to make the tale even more believable,
the church arranged for the sale of indulgences

00:17:28.169 --> 00:17:32.850
while claiming all the money was paying for
some much-needed work on St. Peter's Basilica.

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:36.270
In reality, half of the money went to the
pope, and the rest went to Jakob.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:40.590
There were some major consequences, though:
the sale of indulgences was so unthinkable

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:44.360
to some Catholics that they decided it was
worth breaking from the church over.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:49.470
Martin Luther's Ninety-Five Theses were kick-started
by outrage over the selling of indulgences,

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:51.970
eventually leading to the Protestant Reformation.

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:56.831
That sequence of events is pretty much a perfect
example of Jakob Fugger's influence: he may

00:17:56.831 --> 00:18:01.590
not be a name that everyone recognizes in
the 21st century, but thanks to his financial

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:07.600
support of powerful Kings and Clergymen across
Europe, he completely rewrote world history.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:13.249
A Florin Goes a Long Way

00:18:13.249 --> 00:18:17.870
Jakob Fugger's wheelings and dealings are
too many to list, so perhaps a better way

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:22.450
to talk about the lasting impact he had on
the world isn't just to talk about how he

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:24.820
made his money, but how he spent it.

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:30.609
Let's take the Fuggerei; in 1517, Jakob oversaw
the groundbreaking of what would be a global

00:18:30.609 --> 00:18:31.609
first.

00:18:31.609 --> 00:18:34.929
After years spent amassing a fortune larger
than a single person could spend in a lifetime,

00:18:34.929 --> 00:18:37.049
Jakob wanted to give back to the community.

00:18:37.049 --> 00:18:40.190
To that end, he built a housing complex called
the Fuggerei.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:44.639
It's located in Augsburg, Germany, and it's
entirely accurate to describe it as a city

00:18:44.639 --> 00:18:45.639
within a city.

00:18:45.639 --> 00:18:50.440
Opened in 1520, it was designed to provide
housing to low-income tenants; it includes

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:53.070
147 apartments, 67 houses, and a church.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:58.119
Those who live there had to apply and meet
specific criteria: they had to prove their

00:18:58.119 --> 00:19:02.720
devotion to the Roman Catholic faith, have
no outstanding debt, be struggling to make

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:05.309
ends meet, and have lived in Bavaria for two
years.

00:19:05.309 --> 00:19:07.220
What are the bonuses?

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:10.919
Rent was just 88 cents a year, and three prayers
a day.

00:19:10.919 --> 00:19:15.529
That's impressive, but what's more impressive
is that 500 years later, the Fuggerei still

00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:20.710
welcomes tenants who are in need, and the
rent has remained absolutely unchanged.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:24.509
The effect that Jakob Fugger — and, indeed,
the Fugger family — had on the world is

00:19:24.509 --> 00:19:29.419
impossible to trace, and there's some things
that we only have time to mention in tidbit

00:19:29.419 --> 00:19:30.419
form.

00:19:30.419 --> 00:19:35.950
After Maxmilian's death in 1519, Jakob's support
of Spain's Charles I led to the Habsburg ruler

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:40.509
becoming the Holy Roman Emperor Charles V.
Jakob also loaned a large sum of money to

00:19:40.509 --> 00:19:46.080
Ferdinand Magellan, who took that money and
set off on his famous expedition to circumnavigate

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.080
the globe.

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:50.929
He even set up a news service, in which he
was sent letters from around the world, updating

00:19:50.929 --> 00:19:55.749
him on the economic and political situation
in all his areas of interest — essentially,

00:19:55.749 --> 00:19:57.730
it was a newspaper before newspapers were
a thing.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:00.970
The end of the Fugger family came gradually.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:05.830
In 1512, Jakob brought four of his nephews
on board; with no children of his own, he

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:07.749
knew he needed someone to pass the business
on to.

00:20:07.749 --> 00:20:13.549
It wasn't until 1525 that Jakob the Rich willed
everything to his nephew Anton, with the stipulation

00:20:13.549 --> 00:20:16.440
that a brother and a cousin would be kept
on to advise him.

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:20.190
Jakob died that same year, while unrest swept
across Hungary.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:24.860
Still, the business continued to grow, and
the firm reached its peak in 1546.

00:20:24.860 --> 00:20:29.929
They continued to establish charitable organizations
(like a foundation for the treatment of smallpox),

00:20:29.929 --> 00:20:33.979
but the very thing that had allowed Jakob
to become one of the richest men in the world

00:20:33.979 --> 00:20:36.590
was the same thing that led to the family's
downfall.

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:41.340
They were so closely tied to the Habsburgs
and the fortunes of Hungary that, as trade

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:44.330
worsened, so did the family’s financial
opportunities.

00:20:44.330 --> 00:20:49.049
By 1550 — two years after their withdrawal
from the Hungarian trade market — Anton

00:20:49.049 --> 00:20:54.179
Fugger told his sons to focus on their education
and find their own fortunes in the courts

00:20:54.179 --> 00:20:55.179
of Europe.

00:20:55.179 --> 00:21:01.009
Their Spanish branch was bankrupt by 1557,
and after Anton's death in 1560, other branches

00:21:01.009 --> 00:21:03.289
that had become unprofitable were sold.

00:21:03.289 --> 00:21:08.779
Leases on mines were returned, the family
withdrew from Spain, and in 1658, the firm

00:21:08.779 --> 00:21:10.429
was dissolved completely.

00:21:10.429 --> 00:21:15.580
It may have been the end of a dynasty, but
the Fugger family influence has lasted much

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:27.179
longer than their fortune.

